****

The subtle helium signal barely shows up on the disk, when inspecting the raw reconstruction. 
True, it is very obvious in the flash spectrum, but on the disk it is some barely visible mottling and the densest of filaments. 
Applying continuum subtraction however, reveals the full splendour of this helium line, and makes the imprint of coronal holes rather obvious.

Processing went much further than continuum subtraction. The delta alone yields a grey-ish image, valueable for inspection, 
and familiar from the literature, but it doesn't capture the gist of the disk -- which we now know from the direct images of the He I 10830 line. 
The delta shows a grey background, a bright chromosphere and surface features, but to our intuition, it is not the Sun, it is just data visualisation. 
Hence, the delta was added-multiplied-logarithmed back onto the disk-at-wavelength, the light blanket over the active regions at the limb 
got dampened down, to produce this concept image. 

This concept image, in retrospect, is a bullzeye direct hit, as it is surprisingly true to the spirit of the helium features. 
Back when I worked on the script, I had no idea what a direct image of the He I 10830 would look like, so I used my imagination 
and experience with other wavelengths and processing methods, namely the raw signal-continuum data and the hydrogen delta vs hydrogen epsilon data.



